1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,540
This tiny ball can hit harder than this giant one.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,100
It sounds wrong, isn't it?

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:05,440
But watch this closely.

4
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,060
The big ball is heavier, and so it barely moves.

5
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,200
But this tiny ball?

6
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,940
It's moving ridiculously fast, and when they collide,

7
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:15,680
the tiny ball can have just as much momentum as the giant one.

8
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,580
It sounds crazier when you do this.

9
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,320
Double the tiny ball's speed, and its momentum doubles.

10
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:21,600
Try to double it again.

11
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,940
And now that little ball is carrying four times the momentum it had at the beginning.

12
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,220
So, what actually determines momentum?

13
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:30,700
It's two things, mass and speed.

14
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:32,420
But there's something even more important.

15
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:37,440
When these two objects collide, the momentum doesn't just vanish, it rather moves.

16
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:40,320
One slows down, while the other speeds up.

17
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:42,380
And the total momentum stays the same.

18
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:48,180
So, a tiny object moving fast enough can have more momentum than something hundreds of times heavier.

19
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,400
And that's why you should never judge an object's momentum by its size.

20
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:55,500
Because that tiny ball might be the dangerous one here.

